  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="82bed444-d9f7-4f22-b2c9-b97fe3923db5" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--1-->
  <exercise data-content-id="3bdda842-a202-4cfa-96bf-c80303719329" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="2">
    <problem>
      <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents \(y=(x−2)^2−8\)?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 2. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is found by using the squared term of the vertex form. The value of the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is 2.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.16.2–7.16.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="b9a9a833-8d7c-4a05-be34-ce23d9a12115" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="-8">
    <problem>
      <para>What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents \(y=(x−2)^2−8\)? </para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! -8. In vertex form, the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is the constant. The value of the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is -8.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.16.2–7.16.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="10697901-1d71-4c1e-8aa3-341cded90fed" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="-4">
    <problem>
      <para>Where is the \(y\)-intercept for the equation \(y=(x−2)^2−8\) located?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! -4. Substitute 0 for \(x\) and you get \(y=(0−2)^2−8=4−8=-4\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.16.2–7.16.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="7614b261-dbad-48ee-a025-2f05bc4aa041" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\((4,-4)\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\((4,-4)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((-4,4)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((4,4)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((-4,-4)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which of the following is another point on the graph of the equation \(y=(x−2)^2−8\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \((4,-4)\). The \(y\)-intercept is 2 horizontal units to the left of the vertical line containing the vertex (the axis of symmetry). Using the symmetry of the graph, another point with the same \(y\)-coordinate is located 2 horizontal units to the right of the vertical line containing the vertex.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.16.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="51a7d19d-acae-4276-9c8c-dd6ad3831974" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="Graph A" data-solution-options="[&quot;Graph A&quot;, &quot;Graph B&quot;, &quot;Graph C&quot;, &quot;Graph D&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which graph is a representation of \(y=(x - 6)^2 - 5\)?</para>
      <image height="219" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e98f1e12af7c125e63a0ff37b612b77c75365e21" width="268"/></problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Yes graph A.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.16.3 and 7.16.4.  </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q6-->
  <exercise data-content-id="57ca4658-384a-4e06-88db-22c107551c69" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="The vertex shows the minimum value." data-solution-options="[&quot;The vertex shows the minimum value.&quot;, &quot;The vertex shows the maximum value.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The function \(v\) is defined by \(v(x)=12(x+5)^2−7\).</para>
      <para>Without graphing, determine if the vertex of the graph representing \(v\) shows the minimum or maximum value of the function.</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para> Correct! The vertex shows the minimum value. The \(a\)-value, or the coefficient of the squared term is positive, so the graph opens upward.This means the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is the minimum value of the function. The vertex is at \((-5,-7)\). To verify your response, enter a couple of \(x\) -values on either side of -5 into the function. For example, find \(v(-6)\) and \(v(-4)\). Each time, the output is greater than \(v(-5)\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q7-->
  <exercise data-content-id="88f0ebcf-8946-42f3-9d92-5ccad7f2a77e" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(y=-\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(y=-2(x−6)^2−5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=(x−6)^2−5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=6(x−6)^2−5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=-\\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Choose the equation that matches the graph of parabola D .</para>
      <image height="219" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e98f1e12af7c125e63a0ff37b612b77c75365e21" width="268"/></problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(y=-\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.16.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q8-->
  <exercise data-content-id="7786bff1-c50b-4411-b3e8-1b0fcf3eded0" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(y=-2(x−6)^2−5\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(y=-2(x−6)^2−5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=(x−6)^2−5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=6(x−6)^2−5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=-\\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Choose the equation that matches the graph B.</para>
      <image height="219" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e98f1e12af7c125e63a0ff37b612b77c75365e21" width="268"/></problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.16.3.
      </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--9-->
  <exercise data-content-id="26117eea-4e73-4f7b-8e19-a0eecac697c2" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(y=6(x−6)^2−5\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(y=-2(x−6)^2−5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=(x−6)^2−5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=6(x−6)^2−5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=-\\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Choose the equation that matches the graph C.</para>
      <image height="219" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e98f1e12af7c125e63a0ff37b612b77c75365e21" width="268"/></problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.16.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Mq10-->
  <exercise data-content-id="62388f25-7d7d-4973-9e8b-f8c0eccfc6c4" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(y=-\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(y=-2(x−6)^2−5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=(x−6)^2−5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=6(x−6)^2−5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=-\\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\\)&quot;]">
